Done. `/app/model.xml` now simulates the same 2-second scene in **~46% of the reference time** (2.18× speedup), well under the 60% target.

## What I changed

Only solver/linear-algebra options — no physical properties, geometry, joints, damping, masses, timestep, or plugins were altered:

```xml
<option jacobian="dense" solver="PGS" tolerance="1e-12"/>
```

Why it works:

- **`jacobian="dense"`** — profiling showed the serial cable's mass matrix is ~98% dense, so the default auto/sparse Cholesky was paying fill-in overhead. Dense factorization is cheaper here.
- **`solver="PGS"` with `tolerance="1e-12"`** — the constraint solve (one `connect` equality) dominated runtime under Newton because of its warmstart/factor/solve overhead. PGS converges to the same solution with far less work; the tight tolerance keeps the result essentially exact.

## Validation

- `eval.py` passes repeatedly: state diff prints `0.0000`, time pctg **0.46**.
- Stress-tested **300 random seeds**: maximum absolute state difference **4.25e-8** vs `model_ref.xml` (tolerance is 1e-5, ~235× margin), no NaN/Inf, zero failures.
- `/app/model_ref.xml` is untouched.
